{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"copper-arboresque-pekingese","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nEpic 62dc's criterion 5 is undelivered: plan review does not tell a reviewer that the plan under review overlaps work already described by another ticket. A reviewer reads one plan in isolation, so two tickets that plan the same change both pass, and the duplication is found later in code review or not at all.\n\nCreate-time surfacing (sibling `cleareyed-powellite-wryneck`) catches a duplicate at the moment of filing, when the only evidence is a title and a short description. Plan review runs later against a full plan, which is far more material to match on, and is the last gate before work starts. The same index serves both.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Reuse the index; add no second retrieval path.** The sibling story builds a lexical FTS5 index under `.rebar/cache/similar-tickets/` keyed by `compute_dir_hash`, with synchronous read-phase catch-up and no daemon. This ticket queries it with the plan's own material instead of a new ticket's title. If the optional embedding tier is configured, the query ranks through it; if not, it ranks lexically. Overlap detection therefore inherits the sibling's portability: it works with no model provider configured, and is unavailable in no installation.\n\n**Ride the existing Pass-1 extra-context seam.** A Pass-1 finder unit already accepts store-derived `extra_context`, and its identity binds that context by `context_digest` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/checkpoints.py:52-72`), under the single registered discovery contract `plan_review_findings` (`src/rebar/llm/plan_review/passes.py:122`). Retrieved candidates are injected as that context rather than through a new pass, so checkpoint identity, caching and resumption keep working unchanged — a candidate set that changes produces a different `context_digest` and correctly invalidates the checkpoint, which is the behaviour already specified for injected context.\n\n**The budget is a hard cap, not a target.** At most 10 candidates reach the judge and at most 2 LLM calls are spent, per the epic criterion. Retrieval itself is not an LLM call on the lexical tier, so the common case spends its budget entirely on judging. The cap is asserted by a test rather than documented, because an unasserted budget silently grows.\n\n**On demand, not on every review.** Overlap judging is opt-in per run. A plan review that does not ask for it spends 0 additional calls and its checkpoint identity is unchanged, so turning the feature on cannot alter the cost or the cached results of reviews that do not use it.\n\n**Findings are advisory.** An overlap finding reports the candidate ticket and why it overlaps; it does not BLOCK. A ticket that legitimately follows another — a child, a follow-up, a deliberate split — overlaps by construction, so a blocking verdict would stall correct decompositions. The reviewer decides.\n\n**Degradation matches the sibling's.** When the index is absent, catch-up fails, or the configured embedding provider errors, overlap judging is skipped with a diagnostic and the plan review proceeds to its normal verdict. Retrieval cannot change a review's pass or block outcome by failing.\n\nAny ratcheted mechanism this adds carries a `# mechanism-ok:` marker at its definition site naming this ticket, since `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` fails on any unadmitted new mechanism.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] With overlap judging requested, a plan review of a ticket whose plan restates an existing ticket's plan emits an advisory overlap finding naming that ticket. Checked by: a sidecar test over a seeded store asserting the finding is present and names the expected ticket id.\n- [ ] At most 10 candidates are passed to the judge and at most 2 LLM calls are made for overlap judging, measured on a store where retrieval returns more than 10 candidates. Checked by: the plan-review sidecar test asserting both the candidate count and the LLM call count.\n- [ ] With overlap judging not requested, no overlap finding is emitted, no additional LLM call is made, and the run's checkpoint identity is byte-identical to the same review before this change. Checked by: a test asserting the call count is unchanged and comparing the computed checkpoint identity against the unmodified path.\n- [ ] An overlap finding never changes a review's verdict from pass to block. Checked by: a test asserting the verdict of a passing plan is unchanged when an overlap finding is present.\n- [ ] With no model provider configured and no network, overlap retrieval still returns candidates from the lexical tier. Checked by: a test that clears provider credentials and asserts a non-empty candidate set reaches the judge boundary.\n- [ ] When the index is absent and when catch-up raises, the plan review completes with its normal verdict and records a diagnostic instead of an overlap finding. Checked by: one test per failure mode asserting the verdict and the diagnostic.\n- [ ] Every module added or modified under `src/rebar/` is at most 800 lines, and `python scripts/check_mechanism_delta.py --check` and `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` both exit 0. Checked by: running the two scripts and `make verify`.","id":"a350-2e7e-71eb-4f5b","parent_id":"62dc-535b-5d05-421d","priority":2,"tags":["complexity-reduction"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Surface plan-review overlap findings on demand against the similar-ticket index"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793579383398922712,"uuid":"41091023-2f9a-4f0a-8ba9-f2c264fba68a"}